.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt BENCH_COMPARE.PY 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bench_compare.py
.Nd percentage tables for llama-bench JSON results
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm published
.Ar published.json
.Ar CARD Ns = Ns Ar OURS.json
.Op Ar ...
.Op Fl -format Ar fmt
.Nm
.Cm delta
.Ar a.json
.Ar b.json
.Op Fl -label-a Ar label
.Op Fl -label-b Ar label
.Op Fl -format Ar fmt
.Nm
.Op Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
turns
.Xr llama-bench 1
JSON files into tables with one percentage per row.
It has two modes.
.Pp
In
.Cm published
mode every
.Cm CARD=OURS.json
pair is compared against a published file, which is a JSON object with a
.Cm rows
list.
Every row of that file names a card, a GPU, a backend, an operating system, a
build, the flash-attention setting, the numbers for pp512 and tg128, and a
.Cm compare
flag.
For every card, every flash-attention setting of our file and every test
(pp512, tg128) the row gives our throughput, the median of the published
numbers for that card and setting, the smallest and the largest of them, how
many there were, and the percentage.
.Pp
In
.Cm delta
mode two of our own runs are compared the same way.
.Pp
The percentage of the
.Cm published
mode is (ours − median) / median;
the percentage of the
.Cm delta
mode is (a − b) / b,
where a is the first run and b the second.
The number is signed: a positive number means our run is faster than the
number it is compared with.
It is printed with one decimal, a plus sign for the positive numbers and
U+2212 MINUS SIGN (not the ASCII hyphen) for the negative ones.
.Pp
A published row with
.Cm "compare": false
is printed under "Reference only": it never enters the median, and it carries
its own percentage against our run.
A row without a
.Cm compare
key at all is compared.
The flag must be a JSON boolean: a string, a number or null is an error.
.Pp
A row of a bench file is named from both of its counters: a row that measured
the prompt only is
.Cm ppN ,
a row that measured the generation only is
.Cm tgN ,
and a row that measured both is
.Cm ppNtgM .
The combined row comes from the combined prompt-and-generation test of
.Xr llama-bench 1 ,
.Cm -pg N,M ;
.Cm -p
and
.Cm -n
are the two separate tests.
A file with the same row twice is refused.
.Pp
One of our rows that has no published row for its card, its test and its
flash-attention setting is reported on stderr, one line per row: it is in the
table with an empty median, which says nothing about the run.
This is what a run with the default flash attention of
.Xr llama-bench 1
looks like: that default is AUTO, it is written as
.Cm -1 ,
and a
.Cm -1
row matches no published
.Cm fa
0 or
.Cm fa
1 row.
.Pp
The output format is chosen with
.Cm --format :
.Cm md
(the default),
.Cm json
or
.Cm html .
The labels of the two runs of the
.Cm delta
mode are chosen with
.Cm --label-a
and
.Cm --label-b ;
without them the name of the file is used.
.Cm -h
and
.Cm --help
print the usage and exit 0.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The table was printed.
.It 2
The input was wrong.
One line
.Pq Li "bench_compare.py: ..."
on stderr, never a traceback.
A number that is not finite (NaN, Infinity) is wrong input: no table can hold
it.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Our run against the published numbers for the same card:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bench_compare.py published published.json nv=ours.json
.Ed
.Pp
Our two runs against each other, as an HTML table:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bench_compare.py delta build-a.json build-b.json --format html
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr llama-bench 1
